**1. FILE: `MoveToAndClickAction.java`, new lines ~47-64 (hunk `@@ -43,12 +43,38 @@`).**
The new code scrolls the window and *then* clicks using the *same* `(x, y)` coordinates as viewport coordinates:
```java
page.evaluate("... window.scrollTo({left: scrollX, top: scrollY...})", ...);
page.mouse().move(x, y);
page.mouse().click(x, y);
```
`page.mouse().click(x, y)` takes viewport-relative coordinates, but the scroll only makes sense if `x,y` are *document* coordinates (you scroll to center "the target at (x,y)"). After `scrollTo(scrollX, scrollY)`, the document point (x,y) sits at viewport position `(x - scrollX, y - scrollY)` — the center — not at viewport `(x, y)`. So the click lands at the wrong on-screen spot, or, if `x,y` exceed the viewport, completely off‑screen (thin air / wrong element / nothing). Concrete: `x=1500, y=2000` on an 800×600 page → `scrollTo(1100, 1700)`, then `click(1500, 2000)` attempts an off‑viewport click → wrong element or "element intercepted / times out". This is a regression from the old behavior which did not scroll at all.

**6. FILE: `NavigateAction.java`, new lines ~41-43 (hunk `@@ -38,12 +38,12 @@`).**
```java
if (!url.startsWith("http://") && !url.startsWith("https://")) {
    url = "https://" + url;
}
```
The `startsWith` check is case-sensitive, and it treats *any* string lacking an `http(s)://` prefix as "missing scheme." Concrete: `HTTP://example.com` or `Http://example.com` → prefixed to `https://HTTP://example.com` → navigation fails / wrong host; a `file:`, `data:`, `chrome://`, or `about:` URL, or a relative path like `/login`, also gets `https://` prepended, silently diverting the navigation to an invalid/unintended destination and turning what should have failed (or used the given scheme) into a mangled http request.

---

Note again: the source tree is not present in this workspace, so I verified nothing beyond the diff text itself; line numbers are approximate offsets from the hunk headers. If the repo is material to this review, it should be made available and I'll re-validate these against the live files.

